          "content": [
            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Define $f_{n}:[0,1] \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathbb{R}$ by the equation $f_{n}(x)=x^{n}$. Show that the sequence $\\\\left(f_{n}\\\\right)$ does not converge uniformly.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"topology\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"For each x∈[0,1), we have x^n→0, while f_n(1)=1 for every n. Thus the pointwise limit is\\n\\nf(x)={0 if 0≤x<1, 1 if x=1}.\\n\\nIf (f_n) converged uniformly, its uniform limit would necessarily equal this pointwise limit f. Fix ε=1/2. For every N∈Z_+, choose n=N and x=2^{-1/N}∈[0,1). Then f(x)=0 and\\n\\n|f_n(x)−f(x)|=x^N=1/2.\\n\\nHence, for every N, there exist n≥N and x∈[0,1] such that |f_n(x)−f(x)|≥ε. This contradicts the criterion for uniform convergence. Therefore (f_n) does not converge uniformly on [0,1].\"}",
              "text_elements": [],
